.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt SAFE-AGENT 1
.Os POSIX.1-2017
.Sh NAME
.Nm safe-agent
.Nd decryption key agent
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hdgv
.Op Fl t Ar timeout
.Op Fl f Ar socket
.Op Fl s Ar storedir
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Op Fl p Ar prefix
.Op Fl e Ar peer-exe
.Op Fl c Ar confirm-cmd
.Op Fl S Ar lsm-label
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This is a fork of Willy Goiffon's
.Xr safe-agent 1 ;
see
.Xr safe 1
for a summary of the changes.
.Pp
.Nm
retains a derived key and its salt in memory and serves a
UNIX-domain socket.
It never returns the key: clients send it entry paths and data, and
the agent decrypts or encrypts for them
.Pq see Sx PROTOCOL .
Which client may ask for what is decided by uid, not only by the
socket's file permissions.
.Pp
The retained key can be instantly forgotten by sending
.Dv SIGUSR1
or
.Dv SIGALRM
to the running agent, or with
.Nm safe Fl F .
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGHUP
forget the key, remove the socket, the pid file and the
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/safe/sock
pointer if it names this agent, and, when the socket directory was
created by the agent, that directory as well.
.Pp
The agent prints two
.Xr sh 1
commands to stdout that can be evaluated by the calling shell to export
the connection parameters to the environment:
.Bd -literal
  SAFE_PID=<pid>; export SAFE_PID
  SAFE_SOCK=<path>; export SAFE_SOCK
.Ed
.Pp
Later calls to
.Xr safe 1
will use these environment variables to retrieve the key instead of
prompting for the master password.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h
Print a quick usage text.
.It Fl d
Do not detach the process from the controlling terminal.
No readiness handshake is performed in this mode; the caller is
responsible for waiting for the socket to appear.
.It Fl g
Widen the socket mode from 0600 to 0660 so a peer in the socket's
group can connect.
The parent directory's setgid bit makes the socket inherit the
directory's group.
Use with
.Fl p
to restrict what that group peer can ask for; without
.Fl p
the group peer gets access to the entire store, and the agent prints
a warning at startup.
This is what lets the hardened profile's native messaging host,
running as
.Em _tatl:safe-tatl ,
reach the browser agent.
.It Fl v
Turn on verbose mode.
.Nm
prints debugging messages to stderr.
This is useful to troubleshoot connection issues between the agent
and the client.
In the default (background) mode, stderr is normally redirected to
.Pa /dev/null ;
.Fl v
preserves it.
.It Fl t Ar timeout
Retain the key for
.Ar timeout
seconds, then forget it via
.Xr alarm 2 .
.Ar timeout
must be non-negative; 0 disables the timeout.
A timeout is required: without
.Fl t ,
or with
.Fl t Ar 0 ,
the agent refuses to start unless
.Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_TIMEOUT
is set, and then prints a warning.
.It Fl f Ar socket
Bind the agent to the
.Ar socket
UNIX-domain socket.
The parent directory of
.Ar socket
must be owned by the current user and must not grant write to group
or any access to others (mode 0700 or, in the hardened profile,
0750 with the setgid bit for the shared group).
(default: /tmp/safe-XXXXXX/agent.<pid>)
.It Fl s Ar storedir
Directory containing the encrypted secrets.
The path is resolved with
.Xr realpath 3
and opened with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
at startup; the
.Sq D
command opens files relative to that directory, and
.Xr landlock 2
permits reads beneath it.
(default:
.Ev SAFE_DIR ,
then the compiled-in default,
.Pa .secrets )
.It Fl u Ar uid
Additionally allow the given
.Ar uid
to send
.Sq D .
The agent's own euid is always allowed.
.Sq P ,
.Sq I
and
.Sq E
are never extended by this flag: they are restricted to the agent's
euid regardless.
Used in the hardened profile, where the browser agent runs as
.Em _safe-browser
and its only
.Sq D
client is the setuid native messaging host running as
.Em _tatl .
.It Fl p Ar prefix
Restrict
.Sq D
and
.Sq E
to paths beginning with
.Ar prefix ,
which must end with
.Sq / .
The prefix check runs after the full path validation described
under
.Sx PROTOCOL ;
it is a second check, not the only one.
Omit for an agent that serves the entire store.
.It Fl e Ar peer-exe
Not available in sandboxed builds (the default on Linux x86_64 and
aarch64): the agent refuses to start with it, because seccomp forbids
the
.Xr readlink 2
it needs.
Require the peer's
.Pa /proc/<pid>/exe
to resolve to exactly
.Ar peer-exe
in addition to the uid check.
This uses
.Xr readlink 2
on
.Pa /proc/<pid>/exe ,
which is subject to
.Xr ptrace 2
access checks (same euid, or
.Dv CAP_SYS_PTRACE ) .
In the hardened profile the agent
.Pq Em _safe-browser
and the native messaging host
.Pq Em _tatl
run as different uids, so
.Fl e
will reject every request unless the agent runs as the same uid as
the peer; do not pass
.Fl e
in the hardened profile.
Intended for the default profile, where it closes code execution as
the user through a route other than the installed
.Pa safe-fill-host .
The agent prints a warning at startup if the check cannot work
because the current process cannot read
.Pa /proc/1/exe .
.It Fl c Ar confirm-cmd
Not available in sandboxed builds: the agent refuses to start with it,
because seccomp forbids the
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr execve 2
it needs.
Run
.Ar confirm-cmd
with the requested path as its last argument before every
.Sq D ;
nonzero exit rejects the request.
Runs as the agent's euid.
Off by default.
In the default profile the command runs as the user and can reach
the display; in the hardened profile it runs as
.Em _safe-browser
and will fail unless the command is setuid, which is treated as
"user denied" (fail closed).
The command is split on whitespace; a command that needs to embed
spaces in an argument should be wrapped in a small script.
.It Fl S Ar lsm-label
Require the peer's
.Xr SO_PEERSEC 2
label to equal
.Ar lsm-label .
This is the LSM layer (SELinux, AppArmor, Smack) above
.Xr SO_PEERCRED 2 ;
it has no effect until an LSM policy is installed for the agent and
the peer, and on systems without
.Dv SO_PEERSEC
every peer is rejected, which is the safe direction.
.El
.Sh PROTOCOL
Each connection begins with a single-byte command:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Sy P
Owner only.
Followed by a 48-byte blob (16-byte salt, then a 32-byte
.Xr crypto_secretstream_xchacha20poly1305 3
key) and one flag byte: 0 for a persistent key, 1 for a one-shot key,
forgotten after the next successful
.Sq D
or
.Sq E .
The agent replaces its retained key with the blob, resets its timeout
and answers with a single
.Ql K .
If the 49 bytes do not all arrive within 5 seconds each, or the flag
byte is neither 0 nor 1, nothing is answered and the previously
retained key, if any, is kept.
.It Sy I
Owner only.
The agent replies
.Ql K ,
a status byte (0: no key, 1: the key opens this store's master entry,
2: a key is loaded but is not this store's key), and the store
directory's
.Va st_dev
and
.Va st_ino
as two big-endian 64-bit integers.
Clients use it to check that the agent serves the store they are using.
.It Sy E
Owner only.
Followed by a 4-byte big-endian length and the path.
The agent replies
.Ql K ,
the 40-byte header and the 24-byte stream header.
The client then sends the plaintext in frames: a 4-byte big-endian
length (at most 8192) followed by that many bytes.
A frame shorter than 8192 bytes, possibly empty, is the last one.
The agent answers every frame with its ciphertext chunk; the last,
short frame is padded first, so its reply is the padded size (see
.Xr safe-store 5 )
plus 17 bytes, and every other reply is the frame length plus 17.
The concatenated reply is a complete format 0x12 store entry bound to
its header and the path.
A client reads the version in the returned header: an agent from
before format 0x12 returns version 0x11 and does not pad.
The key never leaves the agent.
The same path rules as for
.Sq D
apply; the master entry is refused, the total is capped at 64 MiB and
the whole exchange at 60 seconds.
.It Sy F
Accepted from the agent's euid and from root.
The agent forgets its key and answers
.Ql K .
Used by
.Nm safe Fl F .
.It Sy D
Accepted from the agent's euid and from the
.Fl u
uid.
Followed by a 4-byte big-endian length and that many bytes of
path, no trailing NUL.
The agent validates the path with the same rules
.Xr safe 1
uses (no absolute paths, no
.Ql .. ,
no
.Ql // ,
no leading
.Ql ./ ,
no trailing
.Ql / ) ,
rejects it if
.Fl p
was given and the path does not begin with the prefix, and opens
it beneath
.Fl s
with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW .
The path is used as AEAD associated data, so a file whose name was
changed by an attacker does not decrypt under its new name.
The whole entry is authenticated before anything is sent.
Entries of format 0x11 and 0x12 are both served; the padding of a 0x12
entry is removed, so the length is that of the stored plaintext.
The reply is
.Ql K ,
a 4-byte big-endian plaintext length and the plaintext; a failure
produces no output at all.
The master entry is verified but its (constant) payload is not
released.
If the agent has no key loaded, the connection is closed without
writing.
.It Sy L
Accepted from the agent's euid and from the
.Fl u
uid.
Followed by a 4-byte big-endian length and a directory path, checked
like a
.Sy D
path.
With
.Fl p
the directory must lie strictly beneath the prefix
.Pq Ql passwords/example.org ,
so a client can list the accounts saved for a site it names but not
the list of sites; without
.Fl p
only the agent's euid may list.
The directory is opened beneath
.Fl s
without following symbolic links and is not descended into.
The reply is
.Ql K ,
a 4-byte big-endian length and the names of the regular files in it
that are valid usernames (see
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ) ,
separated by newlines, in directory order.
At most 256 names and 16 KiB are returned.
A missing directory is an empty list.
Nothing is decrypted, so a name is listed whether or not its entry
authenticates.
If the agent has no key loaded, the connection is closed without
writing, so a locked agent does not disclose names.
Used by
.Xr safe-fill-host 1
to copy a username.
.El
.Pp
Any other command byte is ignored and the connection is closed.
The per-connection deadline is 5 seconds and applies independently of
the
.Fl t
timeout, which governs how long the agent retains a loaded key.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /tmp/safe-XXXXXX/agent.<pid>
Default socket path.
The temporary directory is created with
.Xr mkdtemp 3
mode 0700 and the socket is bound with mode 0600.
.It Pa <socket_dir>/agent.pid
Written by the agent after binding the socket, mode 0640, owned by
the agent's euid.
Contains the agent's pid as a decimal integer followed by a newline,
for scripts and service managers.
The tools in this tree do not read it.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/safe/sock
When
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is set, the agent writes its socket path here, so
.Xr safe 1
.Fl F
and
.Xr safe-fill-host 1
can find it without
.Ev SAFE_SOCK .
Removed on exit if it still names this agent.
.El
.Pp
In the default socket name, <pid> is the pid of the process that was
started; the agent that serves the socket is its forked child, whose
pid is printed as
.Ev SAFE_PID .
With
.Fl d
they are the same.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Retrieve a secret from your safe using the agent:
.Bd -literal
  $ eval $(safe-agent -t 900)
  $ safe -r
  password:
  $ safe secret/file > kitten.gif
.Ed
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "SAFE_SOCK"
.It Ev SAFE_PID
Set by the shell commands printed by
.Nm .
Stores the PID of the running agent.
.It Ev SAFE_SOCK
Set by the shell commands printed by
.Nm .
Stores the path to the UNIX-domain socket used to communicate with the
agent.
.It Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_MLOCK
If set, downgrade
.Xr mlock 2
failures from fatal errors to warnings.
The agent locks only a few tens of kB, so the default limit of most
systems suffices; the hardened setup does not set this variable and
its verify step fails if it is set.
.It Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_TIMEOUT
If set, allow
.Fl t Ar 0 ,
or no
.Fl t ,
to disable the idle backstop.
Without this variable the agent refuses to start without a nonzero
timeout.
.It Ev SAFE_ALLOW_NO_LANDLOCK
If set, run without Landlock when the kernel does not support it
(sandboxed Linux builds only).
.It Ev SAFE_DIR
Store directory when
.Fl s
is not given.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the socket path is published; see
.Sx FILES .
.El
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The agent socket is bound inside a per-agent
.Xr mkdtemp 3
directory with mode 0700.
In the hardened profile the socket lives under
.Pa /run/safe/<store>/
(mode 0750, setgid, owned by the agent's euid) and the socket is
mode 0660, group-inherited from the directory so the shared group
can connect.
Both the socket and its parent directory are validated by
.Xr safe 1
before use: the parent must be owned by the calling user and must
not grant write to group or any access to others, and the socket
itself must be owned by the same user.
.Pp
The agent distinguishes its clients by uid using
.Dv SO_PEERCRED ,
not just by Unix file permissions on the socket.
The
.Sq D
command requires the peer uid to be the agent's own euid, or the
.Ar uid
given with
.Fl u ;
the master entry is only verified for the agent's own euid and is
refused to the
.Fl u
uid.
The
.Sq P ,
.Sq I
and
.Sq E
commands require exactly the agent's euid and are never extended by
.Fl u .
.Sq F
is accepted from the agent's euid and from root.
Thus
.Fl u
grants decrypt access only; there is no command that returns the key.
In the hardened profile this is what confines the setuid native
messaging host to browser passwords; it can ask for decryptions of
.Pa passwords/* ,
but cannot ask for the key.
.Pp
With
.Fl e ,
the peer's executable is checked via
.Pa /proc/<pid>/exe
after
.Xr SO_PEERCRED 2 ;
a mismatch closes the connection before the command byte is read.
This is a default-profile defense only; see
.Fl e
above for the ptrace-access caveat.
.Pp
With
.Fl S ,
the peer's
.Xr SO_PEERSEC 2
label is checked before the command byte is read.
Without an LSM policy the label is generic and the check is
effectively disabled by the empty default; with
.Fl S
set, every peer whose label does not match exactly is rejected.
On systems without
.Dv SO_PEERSEC
the check fails closed: every peer is rejected.
.Pp
With
.Fl c ,
every
.Sq D
is preceded by a confirmation command.
The confirmation runs before the store is opened, so an attacker
cannot probe which paths exist without user approval.
A nonzero exit or a signalled interruption
.Pq Dv SIGUSR1 No for forget, Dv SIGINT/SIGTERM No for quit
is treated as denial.
.Pp
The key is stored in memory that has been
.Xr mlock 2 Ns 'd
with
.Xr sodium_mlock 3 ;
if locking fails, the agent exits unless
.Ev SAFE_UNSAFE_MLOCK
is set.
.Ic safe-hardened-setup
writes systemd or dinit services; pass it
.Fl m Ar bytes
to add a memlock limit to them if the service manager's default
limit is too low.
The derived key and the two secretstream buffers are all
.Nm mlock Ns 'd .
.Pp
When
.Dv SIGUSR1
or
.Dv SIGALRM
is delivered while a
.Sq D
is in progress, the request completes with the key that was loaded
at accept time, and the key is zeroed after the response is written.
The signal is observed at the next iteration of the accept loop.
This is the intended behavior: the request was already authorized
when the signal arrived.
.Pp
On Linux, release builds enable
.Xr seccomp 2
and
.Xr landlock 2
sandboxing and disable core dumps and
.Xr ptrace 2 .
These are disabled when the code is compiled with
.Dv _DEBUG .
.Pp
The agent serves one connection at a time, with a 5-second deadline
for each read (60 seconds for a whole
.Sq E ) .
Any process that may connect to the socket can delay other clients by
that much per connection.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr safe 1 ,
.Xr safe-fill-host 1 ,
.Xr safe-store 5
